parts.I:
MISTAKES: averaging percentages or ratios without weights (Module 26);
   reporting the mean of heavily skewed data (income) as "typical";
   averaging ordinal codes (1-5 satisfaction) without noting the
   assumption.

blocks.rawText:
HISTOGRAM: bars over contiguous bins; bar height shows the COUNT (or
relative frequency) in the bin. Bars touch (the variable is continuous);
contrast with a bar chart (categories, gaps).

parts.E:
CONDITIONS: needs PAIRED data of equal length. Units are (unit of x) x
   (unit of y), so the magnitude is hard to interpret. Properties:
   Cov(aX + b, cY + d) = a c Cov(X, Y);
   Cov(X, Y) = Cov(Y, X); Var(X + Y) = Var X + Var Y + 2 Cov(X, Y);
   independent => Cov = 0 (converse FALSE).

blocks.formulaBody:
C(n, k) = n! / ( k! (n - k)! ) = P(n, k) / k!
     Properties: C(n, k) = C(n, n - k); C(n, 0) = C(n, n) = 1;
        C(n, 1) = n; C(n, k) = C(n-1, k-1) + C(n-1, k) (Pascal);
        sum[k=0..n] C(n, k) = 2^n.
     Multisets (choose k from n types WITH repetition, order irrelevant):
        C(n + k - 1, k)  [SUPPLEMENTARY]

parts.H:
INTERPRETATION: conditioning on information changes probabilities;
   features in a classifier are exactly conditioning information:
   P(y | x).

parts.B:
FORMULAS
     P(X = 1) = p, P(X = 0) = 1 - p;  PMF: p(x) = p^x (1 - p)^(1 - x), x in {0, 1}
     mean E[X] = p;  variance Var(X) = p (1 - p).

parts.F:
DERIVATION: E[X] = 1 * p + 0 * (1 - p) = p; E[X^2] = p (since X^2 = X);
   Var = p - p^2 = p (1 - p). The variance is largest at p = 0.5 (0.25) and
   zero at p = 0 or 1 (no uncertainty).

parts.J:
RELATED: Poisson counts (number of events in time t ~ Poisson(lambda t));
   geometric distribution (discrete analog: trials until first
   success, E = 1/p); gamma distribution (sum of exponentials).

parts.I:
MISTAKES: E[1/X] = 1/E[X] (WRONG); E[X^2] = (E[X])^2 (WRONG; the gap is the
   variance); E[XY] = E[X] E[Y] without independence (WRONG);
   computing E of a continuous variable by summing.

MISTAKES: Var(a X) = a Var(X) (WRONG: a^2); adding standard deviations;
   Var(X - Y) = Var X - Var Y (WRONG: variances ADD for independent
   differences); applying Var(sum) = sum Var to dependent variables;
   using population and sample formulas interchangeably (Module 31).

parts.J:
RELATED: change of variables (Module 13); z-scores (Module 35);
   feature scaling and Box-Cox/log transforms; the delta method:
   Var(g(X)) approx (g'(mu))^2 sigma^2 for small sigma [SUPPLEMENTARY].

blocks.formulaBody:
for iid observations x_1..x_n from a density (or PMF) f(x; theta):
        L(theta) = prod[i=1..n] f(x_i; theta)
   Log-likelihood:  l(theta) = ln L(theta) = sum[i=1..n] ln f(x_i; theta).

parts.E:
WHY THE LOGARITHM
   (1) ln is increasing, so L and l have the SAME maximizer;
   (2) it turns the product of n factors into a sum: far easier to
       differentiate (F3.8) and add;
   (3) numerical stability: a product of 1,000 small densities underflows
       to 0, a sum of logs does not;
   (4) the negative log-likelihood is the usual LOSS function (cross-entropy
       for classification, squared error for Gaussian regression).
   Independence is what makes L a product; for dependent data use the
   joint density (chain rule).

CONDITIONS: identifiability can be global or local;
   near-non-identifiability (weak identification) is also harmful in
   practice.

parts.I:
COMMON MISTAKES: see the list 'What a p-value is not' in this
   section.
COMMON MISTAKES: the dichotomy p = 0.049 "significant" vs p = 0.051 "not";
   reporting only "p < 0.05" without estimate and interval; p-hacking
   (trying many analyses and reporting the one with p < 0.05);
   one-sided p after peeking.

parts.C:
SYMBOLS: S_xx = sum (x_i - x_bar)^2; S_xy = sum (x_i - x_bar)(y_i - y_bar);
   r the correlation (Module 32); s_x, s_y sample SDs; n - 2 degrees of
   freedom (two parameters estimated).

blocks.formulaBody:
SE(b_1) = sigma_hat / sqrt(S_xx);
   SE(b_0) = sigma_hat sqrt( 1/n + x_bar^2/S_xx );
   t-statistic for H0: beta_j = 0:  t = b_j / SE(b_j) ~ t_(n - 2) under H0;
   (1 - alpha) CI: b_j +/- t_(alpha/2, n - 2) SE(b_j).
   Overall F-test (simple regression): F = t^2 = MSR/MSE with (1, n - 2) df.

parts.B:
SE(b_1) = sigma_hat / sqrt(S_xx);
   SE(b_0) = sigma_hat sqrt( 1/n + x_bar^2/S_xx );
   t-statistic for H0: beta_j = 0:  t = b_j / SE(b_j) ~ t_(n - 2) under H0;
   (1 - alpha) CI: b_j +/- t_(alpha/2, n - 2) SE(b_j).
   Overall F-test (simple regression): F = t^2 = MSR/MSE with (1, n - 2) df.

parts.C:
SYMBOLS: x_0 the new predictor value; y_hat_0 the prediction;
   sigma_hat the residual standard error; t the critical value with n -
   2 df.
